    37	## For the business
    38	
    39	The adapter is the part that reads an adopter's code, so it decides what
    40	running sealmap costs and whether it can be trusted on a real repository.
    41	Three properties come from this skeleton. It never compiles anything, so it
    42	runs on any checkout, including one that does not build, and needs no
    43	toolchain at run time. It never fails a whole run because of one bad file:
    44	a file that does not parse, or that trips a bug, becomes a warning and a
    45	placeholder entry. And it is fast enough to sit in CI: the README records
    46	1.13 s for a 934-file workspace (`README.md:247`).
    47	
    48	The residual risk is a file nested deeply enough to exhaust even the large
    49	stack: that still ends the process, because a stack overflow cannot be
    50	caught.
    51	
    52	## EXT-01.1 One call from sources to model
    53	
    54	```mermaid
    55	sequenceDiagram
   145	    MI->>RP: build pool with stack_size (isolate.rs:52)
   146	    alt pool built
   147	        RP->>CF: each job under catch_unwind (isolate.rs:46)
   148	    else pool refused
   149	        MI->>MI: one scoped thread with the same stack (isolate.rs:56)
   150	    end
   151	    CF-->>MI: RawFile, or a panic
   152	    MI->>FF: on panic, message as the error (isolate.rs:47)
   153	    FF-->>MI: module-only RawFile tagged parse_error (collect.rs:74)
   154	    MI-->>CA: results in input order (isolate.rs:53)
   155	    Note over RP: DEBT: a stack overflow is an abort,<br/>not a panic, and ends the run, isolate.rs:8
   156	```
   157	
   158	**What it shows.** Every file is collected on a thread with a 64 MiB stack;
   159	a panic in one file becomes a placeholder `RawFile` whose error becomes a
   160	diagnostic, and the run continues.
   161	
   162	**Why it is this way.** The VisionClaw stack overflow came from deeply nested
   163	generics on rayon's default 2 MiB stacks (`docs/DESIGN.md:163`); syn costs
   164	about 40 KiB per nesting level in a debug build
   165	(`crates/sealmap-extract/src/isolate.rs:3`-`7`). Address space is reserved,
   166	not memory, so the large stack is cheap (`crates/sealmap-extract/src/isolate.rs:27`-`29`).
   167	The flow walker's own depth cap is the second guard
   168	(`crates/sealmap-rust/src/collect.rs:30`).
   169	
   170	**Invariant:** behaviour does not depend on the `parallel` feature: without
   171	it, or if the pool cannot be built, the same stack size is used on one scoped
   172	thread (`crates/sealmap-extract/src/isolate.rs:34`-`37`).
